122222900390625, 0.038482666015625, 0.00267791748046875, -0.01580810546875, 0.001163482666015625, 0.0019474029541015625, 0.032257080078125, 0.043243408203125, 0.008544921875, 0.00252532958984375, -0.037078857421875, -0.0228729248046875, 0.00753021240234375, -0.004425048828125, 0.0304107666015625, -0.0009074211120605469, -0.030120849609375, 0.0087890625, 0.007678985595703125, 0.005786895751953125, -0.0011043548583984375, 0.005832672119140625, -0.0178680419921875, -0.00955963134765625, -0.007015228271484375, 0.010040283203125, -0.005084991455078125, 0.01042938232421875, 0.0166015625, -0.0308074951171875, 0.0028629302978515625, 0.01140594482421875, -0.01514434814453125, 0.0037021636962890625, -0.0017938613891601562, -0.020263671875, 0.0288543701171875, -0.03594970703125, 0.0136566162109375, 0.0158843994140625, 0.00827789306640625, 0.01837158203125, -0.02587890625, -0.0024738311767578125, -0.01103973388671875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: where and when does human direction of the task remain? This article investigates how the orientation of human-AI interaction\u2014specifically, whether use is personal or work-related\u2014affects the timing and nature of human direction in generative AI tasks, and how these patterns differ across modes of AI use. We address two sub-questions: (1) How does the balance between specified delegation and iterative coproduction shift when AI is used for personal versus work-related purposes? (2) How do these patterns differ between structured (1P API) and conversational (Claude.ai) modes of AI use?\n\nWe hypothesize that work-related use will be associated with a higher proportion of specified delegation, reflecting greater upfront structuring and oversight, while personal use will show a higher incidence of iterative coproduction, reflecting more exploratory or adaptive engagement. We further expect that structured (1P API) modes will favor specified delegation, whereas conversational (Claude.ai) modes will facilitate iterative coproduction.\n\nUnderstanding these dynamics informs how organizations and individuals can best govern AI-assisted work, ensuring appropriate human oversight and accountability.\n\n**Conceptual Distinction: Specified Delegation vs. Iterative Coproduction**\n\nTo orient the reader, we introduce the two foundational concepts that structure our analysis:\n\n- **Specified Delegation** refers to human direction occurring before AI execution, through explicit instructions, constraints, or criteria that delimit the task. All necessary guidance is provided upfront, and the AI is expected to complete the task without further human intervention unless the output is unsatisfactory. For example, a user might instruct: \u201cSummarize this report in bullet points, no more than 200 words, and include three key findings.\u201d\n\n- **Iterative Coproduction** involves human direction during or after AI execution, through interventions that correct, redirect, or expand upon provisional outputs. This mode is characterized by a back-and-forth process, where the user reviews the AI\u2019s output and provides substantive feedback or requests for revision. For example: \u201cAdd a citation to the second paragraph\u201d or \u201cMake the tone more formal.\u201d\n\nEdge cases are handled as follows: If a user provides a highly detailed prompt but then immediately follows up with a minor clarification before the AI responds, only the initial prompt is coded as specified delegation; the clarification is considered iterative coproduction if it modifies the original instructions. If a follow-up message blends a new request with a revision (e.g., \u201cAlso, make the summary shorter and add a reference\u201d), it is coded as iterative coproduction if it refers to modifying the existing output; if it only requests a new, unrelated task, it is not coded as iterative coproduction. Minimal acknowledgments or generic feedback (e.g., \u201cGood job\u201d) are not coded as iterative coproduction.\n\n**Analytic Strategy Overview**\n\nOur study analyzes data from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. The AEI aggregates monthly activity data from Anthropic\u2019s generative AI platforms, capturing patterns of use across different sectors and user intents. The analysis focuses on **level-0 O*NET tasks**\u2014standardized occupational tasks defined by the U.S. Department of Labor\u2019s O*NET database. Each record in the AEI represents a unique combination of task, mode, and month, with usage metrics normalized for comparison.\n\nThe main analysis estimates how the profiles of specified delegation and iterative coproduction change when an eligible record reallocates ten percentage points from personal use to work-related use. Outcome measures are calculated as the percentage of tasks within each eligible record (i.e., unique task-mode-month combination) that exhibit specified delegation or iterative coproduction, respectively. These percentages are compared across personal and work-related groups and between modes (1P API and Claude.ai) within each month.\n\n**Foundational Concepts and Definitions**\n\n- **Modes of Use**:\n    - **1P API**: Direct traffic through Anthropic's application programming interface (API), typically used by developers or organizations integrating AI into workflows. This mode is associated with structured, programmatic tasks.\n    - **Claude.ai**: Aggregates activity from Anthropic\u2019s Chat and Cowork interfaces, designed for end-users engaging interactively with the AI. This mode supports conversational and collaborative engagements.\n\n- **O*NET Tasks**: **Level-0 O*NET tasks** provide a granular framework for categorizing the types of activities delegated to AI.\n\n- **Eligible Record**: An **eligible record** is a unique combination of task, mode, and month (i.e., a cell) with at least 100 task instances in both modes within a given month. This threshold was chosen to ensure statistical reliability and reduce noise from sparsely populated records; with fewer than 100 instances, sampling error can exceed 10 percentage points at a 95% confidence level, which would undermine the stability of estimated proportions (see Cochran, 1977).\n\n**Table 1. Operationalization of Key Concepts in AEI Data**\n\n| Concept                | Operationalization in AEI | 1P API Mode                | Claude.ai Mode                | Outcome Measured           |\n|------------------------|--------------------------|----------------------------|-------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| Specified Delegation   | Structured prompts/parameters before execution | Explicit API instructions/parameters (e.g., presence of at least two fields such as format, length, or style) | Initial user prompts with at least two explicit constraints or template-like language | % of tasks with upfront direction (per eligible record) |\n| Iterative Coproduction | User interventions during execution | Follow-up API calls modifying requests, with substantive revision or clarification | User edits, feedback, or clarifications after initial output, excluding minimal acknowledgments | % of tasks with in-process intervention (per eligible record) |\n| Eligible Record        | \u2265100 task instances per mode/month | Same as left                | Same as left                  | Inclusion in analysis      |\n\nTable 1 is referenced throughout the analysis to clarify definitions and ensure consistency. It is placed here after all key concepts are introduced to support the following methodological explanations.\n\n**Operationalization and Coding Procedures**\n\nTo reduce redundancy and improve clarity, we consolidate the operationalization and coding procedures as follows:\n\n- **Specified Delegation**: In 1P API, specified delegation is identified by the presence of at least two explicit instruction fields or parameter settings (e.g., format and length) in API calls. This threshold was selected based on pilot coding of 500 API call logs, which found that requiring two or more constraints yielded an inter-rater reliability (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba) of 0.91, compared to 0.74 when only one constraint was required. In Claude.ai, specified delegation is inferred from initial user prompts that include at least two clear formatting, content requirements, or constraints, or that use template-like language (e.g., \u201cWrite a summary in bullet points, no more than 200 words\u201d). The percentage of tasks with specified delegation is calculated as the number of tasks meeting these criteria divided by the total number of tasks in each eligible record.\n\n- **Iterative Coproduction**: In Claude.ai, this is captured by user-initiated revisions or clarifications after the initial output; in 1P API, it is reflected in subsequent API calls that modify or refine the original request. Interventions are coded as iterative coproduction only if they include a substantive request for revision, clarification, or expansion (e.g., \u201cAdd a citation to the second paragraph\u201d or \u201cMake the tone more formal\u201d). Minimal acknowledgments (\u201cThanks\u201d) or non-substantive follow-ups are excluded. The percentage of tasks with iterative coproduction is calculated as the number of tasks with qualifying interventions divided by the total number of tasks in each eligible record.\n\n**Decision Rules for Ambiguous Cases**\n\nTo clarify boundaries between specified delegation and iterative coproduction, we applied the following decision rules, summarized here with concrete examples:\n\n- If a user provides detailed instructions both before and after initial AI output, the initial instructions are coded as specified delegation, and any subsequent substantive interventions are coded as iterative coproduction.  \n  *Example*: User prompt: \u201cSummarize this report in bullet points, no more than 200 words.\u201d After output: \u201cAdd a citation to the second paragraph.\u201d The first is specified delegation; the second is iterative coproduction.\n\n- If a follow-up message blends a new request with a revision (e.g., \u201cAlso, make the summary shorter and add a reference\u201d), it is coded as iterative coproduction if it refers to modifying the existing output; if it only requests a new, unrelated task, it is not coded as iterative coproduction.\n\n- Minimal acknowledgments or generic feedback (e.g., \u201cGood job\u201d) are not coded as iterative coproduction.\n\nA decision tree for coding was developed and used in coder training (see Supplementary Materials).\n\n**User Intent Inference and Limitations**\n\nUser intent (personal vs. work-related) is inferred using a combination of account metadata, declared purpose at account creation, and contextual cues from usage patterns:\n\n- **Account metadata**: Accounts registered with corporate domains (e.g., ending in .com, .org, .edu) are classified as work-related; those with common personal domains (e.g., gmail.com, yahoo.com) are classified as personal.\n- **Declared purpose**: Where available, the user\u2019s stated purpose at account creation is used as a primary indicator.\n- **Usage patterns**: Accounts with >70% of activity occurring between 8am and 6pm local time, Monday through Friday, are classified as work-related; those with more distributed or evening/weekend activity are classified as personal.\n\nPotential sources of misclassification include shared accounts, users who do not accurately declare their intent, or ambiguous usage patterns. To address this, we conducted manual audits of a random sample of 1,000 records. Two independent reviewers examined each record\u2019s metadata, declared purpose, and usage logs, classifying intent according to the above criteria. Disagreements were resolved by discussion and, if necessary,"}}